# FactLattice Abstract Model [SPEC:FL-MODEL]

> A `.lts` document is a container that serializes a fragment of a typed knowledge graph.
> The abstract model below is what every parser must produce; the surface syntax is a
> detail of one serialization. Two documents that produce the same nodes, edges and
> properties are the same knowledge regardless of how they were written.

---

## [MODEL-NODE] Node — the unit of addressable knowledge

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- see-also: [MODEL-SYNTAX]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

A node is one addressable assertion, concept, requirement, component, decision, step, or
observation. It carries an id, a type prefix derived from that id, a title, the document
tag of its containing document, a source location (file plus line range), a content hash,
and a property map.

Nodes are minted by headings that carry a bracketed id, and by list items that define an
id explicitly. A heading with no bracketed id is a **section divider**: it structures the
document and bounds node content, but it is not knowledge and mints nothing. This is the
mechanism by which a document can carry narrative scaffolding without polluting the graph.

Node content is bounded by the next heading of any level. That boundary is what makes a
node's `content_hash` stable under edits elsewhere in the same file — which in turn is
what lets [MECH-INCREMENTAL] re-parse one file and leave the rest of the corpus alone.

---

## [MODEL-ID] Identity — uppercase, prefixed, corpus-unique

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- depends-on: [MODEL-NODE]
- see-also: [MODEL-REF]
- status: draft

Node ids match `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_\-]*$`. The uppercase requirement is not cosmetic: it is
the mechanical signal that separates an id from ordinary bracketed prose, so a parser can
scan a document for references without inference. A lowercase bracket is prose and is
ignored.

The segment before the first hyphen is the **type prefix** (`INV-FRESH` has prefix `INV`).
Prefixes are the primary coarse taxonomy of a corpus: they make `find(prefix="INV")` a
meaningful query without any schema declaration. Which prefixes exist and what they mean
is domain vocabulary, registered in a profile — see [PROFILE-CONTRACT].

Ids are unique per corpus. A duplicate id is an error, not a merge: the first definition
wins and the collision is reported. Silently merging two nodes that happened to share a
name would make every downstream traversal wrong in a way no query could detect.

---

## [MODEL-PROPERTY] Property — typed key/value on a node

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- depends-on: [MODEL-NODE]
- status: draft

Properties are lowercase-keyed key/value pairs attached to a node. Five surface forms
exist (simple, bold-key, multi-value, nested list, and nested mapping); all five produce
the same abstract property map. A repeated key accumulates into a list rather than
overwriting, so a node may carry several `depends-on` declarations without a syntax for
arrays.

A property VALUE is a scalar, a list, or a mapping. The mapping form is what the
`{change, context_for_change}` primitive, the `{task, context_for_task}` primitive and
the `confidence` annotation are specified in — an indented block of unbulleted
`subkey: value` lines under a `- key:` head — and its sub-fields are keyed, queryable
fields, not text inside a list item. Notation Freedom is the reason: a YAML block is a
declared notation, and the parser must extract the same abstract graph whichever
notation an author chose. There is no nesting below one level: a mapping's values are
scalars, and a block that mixes bulleted items with unbulleted entries under one key is
reported as an ambiguity rather than resolved by guessing which half the author meant.

Properties fall into three functional classes, and the class is determined by the key, not
by the value:

- **Descriptive.** `status`, `owner`, `as-of`, `confidence`, and any domain key. Stored,
  queryable, filterable, aggregatable. No graph effect.
- **Relationship-bearing.** `part-of`, `depends-on`, `implements`, `see-also`,
  `supersedes`, `resolves`, `contrast-with`, `used-by`, `issuer`, `tested-by`. Every
  bracketed reference in the value mints a typed edge — see [MODEL-EDGE].
- **Flow-bearing.** `next-step`, `if-true`, `if-false`, `branches`, `converges-to`. Mint
  labelled temporal edges. Their companions `condition` and `actor` are descriptive: they
  change how a node is *drawn* by the RENDER layer but mint nothing.

The split matters because it makes the graph auditable. Any edge in a FactLattice corpus
came from exactly one of: a relationship-bearing property, a flow-bearing property, an
explicit relationships section, an explicit causal-graph section, or heading nesting.
There is no sixth source and no heuristic source.

---

## [MODEL-EDGE] Edge — a declared, typed relationship

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- depends-on: [MODEL-NODE]
- see-also: [MODEL-PROPERTY]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

An edge has a source id, a target id, a type, a layer, an **inference class**, a source
location, and optionally a label. The inference class records how the edge came to exist:

| Inference class | Origin | Example |
|-----------------|--------|---------|
| `property` | A relationship-bearing or flow-bearing property | `- depends-on: [MODEL-NODE]` |
| `authored` | An explicit relationships or causal-graph section line | `- [A] DEPENDS_ON [B]` |
| `structural` | Heading nesting (parent contains child) | An `h3` inside an `h2` |

Recording the inference class is what lets tooling weigh edges differently without
re-parsing. The orphan check in [LAYER-LINT], for instance, counts only `authored` and
`property` edges: if `structural` edges counted, every nested node would trivially be
"referenced" by its own parent heading and the check would find nothing.

Edges are **preserved and flagged, never dropped**. An edge whose target does not exist in
the corpus is retained and marked dangling. An edge whose type carries an unregistered
namespace is retained and flagged. A format that silently discarded what it could not
resolve would make a corpus quietly lose knowledge as it was edited; flagging turns the
same situation into a lint finding.

---

## [MODEL-CANONICAL] CANONICAL — a legacy name pointing at its canonical node, not supersession

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- depends-on: [MODEL-EDGE]
- status: draft
- importance: important

The relationship-bearing key `canonical-node` mints a **CANONICAL** edge (semantic layer) that
runs from the node carrying it to the node it names: legacy to canonical. The bare key
`canonical` does NOT mint: it is already free-text status prose in existing corpora
(`canonical: true -- cutover accepted...`), and minting it would turn that prose into an
edge asserting the opposite of its text. It says "this node
describes the same thing as the target under a non-canonical name or number; the target is
the canonical definition". The edge TYPE name `CANONICAL` is final: tooling that annotates a
legacy node keys on it.

It is **not** `superseded-by`, and the two must not be confused. `superseded-by` (a reversed
SUPERSEDES edge) says the node has been replaced and its content is retired. A CANONICAL
edge says nothing of the kind: the legacy node's content stays valid, and only the preferred
name and number differ. Authoring `superseded-by` where `canonical-node` is meant tells every
reader the content is dead; authoring `canonical-node` where `superseded-by` is meant tells them
a retired node is still good.

Five rules are linted, each at error severity. The value must be refs only: well-formed
bracketed ids and separators, so prose, an unbracketed id or a malformed bracket is an
error rather than silently stored text. The target must exist (a cross-corpus
reference resolves by the existing cross-corpus conventions). A node has at most one
canonical target, since two is ambiguous. A node cannot be its own canonical. The canonical
chain has no cycle.

---

## [MODEL-LAYER] Graph layers — semantic, temporal, causal, entity

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- depends-on: [MODEL-EDGE]
- see-also: [DEC-FOUR-LAYERS]
- status: draft

Every edge belongs to exactly one of four layers. The layer is derived from the edge type,
or set explicitly by the subheading of a relationships section.

| Layer | Answers | Representative types |
|-------|---------|----------------------|
| Semantic | What is this, and what is it part of? | `IS_A`, `PART_OF`, `IMPLEMENTS`, `DEPENDS_ON`, `SUPERSEDES`, `RELATED_TO` |
| Temporal | What comes before what? | `BEFORE`, `AFTER`, `SEQUENCE`, `CONCURRENT`, `VALIDITY_WINDOW` |
| Causal | What makes what happen? | `CAUSES`, `ENABLES`, `PREVENTS`, `PRECONDITION_FOR`, `CASCADES_TO` |
| Entity | Who holds, owns, issues, contains? | `CONTAINS`, `OWNED_BY`, `ISSUES`, `USED_BY`, `TESTED_BY` |

Four layers is a fixed part of core. It is the one piece of vocabulary a domain cannot
extend, because cross-domain traversal is only possible if every domain agrees on what
kind of question a given edge answers. A domain may add edge *types* freely; it places
them in one of these four layers.

Layers are the primary filter on traversal. "How does this process run?" is a temporal
walk. "What breaks if I change this?" is a causal and semantic walk. Asking both at once
returns a hairball; the layer filter is what makes a bounded, legible answer possible.

---

## [MODEL-REF] References — local, doctype-qualified, cross-corpus

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- depends-on: [MODEL-ID]
- status: draft

A reference is a bracketed id in a property value or a relationship line. Three forms are
recognized:

| Form | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `[NODE-ID]` | A node in this corpus |
| `[DOCTYPE:NODE-ID]` | A document root, addressed by its document tag |
| `[domain.suffix:DOCTYPE:NODE-ID]` | A node in another registered corpus |

Cross-corpus references are namespaced, never filesystem paths. A path binds a corpus to
one machine's directory layout and breaks the moment the corpus is published, vendored, or
mounted somewhere else. A namespace binds it to a registered identity that the engine
resolves at query time through its corpus registry; an unresolvable one returns a plain
`unresolved` marker rather than a fabricated node.

---

## [MODEL-DOCTYPE] Document tags and frontmatter

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- depends-on: [MODEL-NODE]
- see-also: [MODEL-CHANGE]
- status: draft

A document opens with a frontmatter block and a single `h1` carrying a
`[DOCTYPE:DOC-ID]`. The doctype tags what kind of document this is — `SPEC`, `SYSTEM`,
`DECISION`, `DOMAIN`, `TESTPLAN`, `DELTA` — and the doc id makes the document itself an
addressable node, so a reference can point at a whole document rather than a section.

Frontmatter carries document-level metadata: `title`, `id`, `domain`, `scope`,
`boundary`, `not-this`, `status`, `created`, `updated`. It mints no edges. `scope`,
`boundary` and `not-this` exist for a specific reason: they let a later reader — human or
machine — decide whether a question belongs in this document without reading the document.
On a large corpus that is the difference between "search every file" and "read five
frontmatter blocks", and it is what makes overlap detection across an existing corpus
tractable at all.

---

## [MODEL-CHANGE] Knowledge evolution — change and context_for_change

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- see-also: [MODEL-PROPERTY]
- status: draft
- importance: high

FactLattice has one first-class primitive for recording how understanding changed:

```yaml
change: "What changed"
context_for_change:
  prior_state: "What was believed before"
  reasoning: "Why it changed"
  rejected_alternative: "What else was considered"
  counter_argument: "The strongest argument against"
  counter_response: "Why that argument does not hold"
  invalidating_assumption: "The premise whose change reopens this"
  implication: "Downstream effects"
```

`invalidating_assumption` is the load-bearing field. It converts a decision from a
conclusion into a conditional: *this holds while X holds*. A future reader can then tell
"still valid, defend it" from "the premise moved, reopen it" without reconstructing the
original argument.

This is the mechanism that closes the failure mode where one Intelligence, lacking the
reasoning behind a prior decision, is used to argue against it — and the argument is
unanswerable because the reasoning was never written down. The reasoning is durable here
because it is a property of a node, queryable like any other.

---

## [MODEL-SYNTAX] Worked example — the concrete syntax

- part-of: [FL-MODEL]
- see-also: [MODEL-EDGE]
- status: draft

A minimal document. The `h1` mints the document node; each `h2` mints a node; each
relationship-bearing property mints an edge. (Shown as an indented block so this
document's own parse is not perturbed by the example's property lines.)

    ---
    title: "Order Service"
    id: "SPEC:ORDER-SERVICE"
    domain: ACME.SERVICES
    ---
    # Order Service [SPEC:ORDER-SERVICE]

    ## [REQ-IDEMPOTENT] Order submission is idempotent
    - status: accepted
    - priority: must
    - tested-by: [TEST-DUPLICATE-SUBMIT]

    ## [MOD-ORDER-API] The order API module
    - implements: [REQ-IDEMPOTENT]
    - path: services/order/api.py

That fragment yields three nodes (one document node plus two `h2` nodes) and four edges:
`MOD-ORDER-API IMPLEMENTS REQ-IDEMPOTENT` from the `implements` property;
`TEST-DUPLICATE-SUBMIT TESTED_BY REQ-IDEMPOTENT` from the reversed `tested-by` property;
and two structural `CONTAINS` edges from the document node. `TEST-DUPLICATE-SUBMIT` is
defined nowhere in the fragment, so its edge is preserved, marked dangling, and reported
by [LAYER-LINT] — which is exactly the behaviour that makes a half-written spec visible
instead of quietly incomplete.

---

*FL-MODEL — the abstract model. Reference material for authoring; read [FL-ENGINE] to see
what the model buys at query time.*
